path, node.save returns nil and node.saveDone stores a completed save task via notifySnapshotStatus(save=true) and signals applyReady (node.go:846-850; snapshotstate.go:185-211), and the apply worker calls processStatusTransition before handleTask, so processSaveStatus clears the saving flag and later periodic snapshots remain enabled (engine.go:1200-1212; node.go:1451-1467); (4) the (0, nil) paths inside doSave are the soft-error case, the abort case that already reported aborted through pendingSnapshot.apply, and the already-up-to-date early return, in which node.save reports the pending user request as rejected (node.go:693-760; request.go:648-668). Counterevidence considered: a hard error bypasses the pendingSnapshot report for that request, but since the process panics at the same point, no client-visible state is retained in a live node, so it cannot strand later snapshot requests. Limitations: this is a source argument, not an execution; it does not cover a state machine whose Save blocks indefinitely (no error at all), nor storage faults inside snapshotter.commit that return errors handled elsewhere, nor whether process termination is an acceptable recovery policy versus returning an error to the caller. The claim is limited to the periodic-save control flow in this snapshot.\",\n \"answers\": {\n  \"9bf8b80283c042d1b383e211c89c1701-gap-1\": {\n   \"explanation\": \"No such state survives. A non-soft error makes node.save return an error, and ssWorker.save panics on that error with no recover around workerMain/handle (engine.go:275-305; the engine recover at 1029-1044 is restricted to step workers under error injection and nodehost.go's recover wraps only NodeHost.Start), so the node does not continue with the saving flag set or an advanced requested index. On the non-fatal paths the flag is cleared: node.save returns nil, saveDone records a completed save task (node.go:846-850; snapshotstate.go:185-211), and the apply worker's processStatusTransition/processSaveStatus clears saving before executing the next task (engine.go:1200-1212; node.go:1451-1467). n.ss.reqSnapshotIndex does not need an explicit reset for recovery because saveSnapshotRequired compares applied against SnapshotEntries plus the requested index, so the next request occurs once applied advances by a further SnapshotEntries window; only restart resets it, and restart also resets the process that would have carried a stale session. The gap is resolved as a refutation of the stranding hypothesis.\",\n   \"resolved\": true\n  }\n },\n \"knowledge\": [\n  {\n   \"entry_id\": \"9bf8b80283c042d1b383e211c89c1701-knowledge-0\",\n   \"revision\": 1,\n   \"assertion\": \"Hard save errors are returned to the caller of node.save, while abort-class and soft errors are handled inside doSave.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"entry_id\": \"9bf8b80283c042d1b383e211c89c1701-knowledge-1\",\n   \"revision\": 1,\n   \"assertion\": \"The periodic snapshot trigger depends on SnapshotEntries, the snapshot index, the requested index and the busy-snapshotting check.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"entry_id\": \"599c7a85d804468187c5a652986302d1-knowledge-0\",\n   \"revision\": 1,\n   \"assertion\": \"A hard save error panics the snapshot worker, ending the process rather than stranding in-process snapshot state.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"entry_id\": \"599c7a85d804468187c5a652986302d1-knowledge-1\",\n   \"revision\": 1,\n   \"assertion\": \"Non-fatal save outcomes complete through saveDone and processSaveStatus, clearing the saving flag.\"\n  }\n ],\n \"counterevidence\": [\n  \"A hard save error bypasses the pendingSnapshot.apply report for that request, so a user-requested snapshot never receives a rejected/aborted result on that path; the process panic is what prevents this from becoming a live stuck request.\"\n ],\n \"limitations\": [\n  \"Source argument only; no CheckRun receipt.\",\n  \"Does not cover a Save implementation that blocks without returning, or storage faults handled outside node.doSave.\",\n  \"Does not judge whether terminating the process on a save error is an acceptable policy; only that it prevents a silent in-process stranding of the periodic snapshot duty.\",\n  \"Does not quantify how much log growth can accumulate between the requested-index advance and the next eligible periodic snapshot.\"\n ],\n \"joint_history\": \"\",\n \"failure_attribution\": null,\n \"operation_id\": \"599c7a85d804468187c5a652986302d1\"\n}\n==========\n{\n \"id\": \"a9985060b7284b23b73850ed64c83991-interpretation-0\",\n \"investigation_id\": \"a9985060b7284b23b73850ed64c83991-question-0\",\n \"experiment_id\": null,\n \"source_ids\": [\n  \"engine_ssworker_handle\",\n  \"engine_worker_launch\",\n  \"engine_pool_worker_launch\",\n  \"engine_error_injection_recover\"\n ],\n \"executions\": {},\n \"conclusion\": \"unresolved\",\n \"defect_claim\": null,\n \"rationale\": \"Current state: the target-side facts are established and the wrapper behaviour was checked outside the target sources. The engine launches its workers through a stopper's RunWorker (engine.go:250-260, 352-364), and the snapshot worker's save path calls panic(err) with no recover in workerMain or handle (engine.go:275-305). The only recover in engine.go is installed around step workers when error injection is enabled (engine.go:1029-1044), and NodeHost's recover wraps Start. The worker wrapper used by RunWorker lives in the goutils dependency; its stopper implementation was read directly and calls the worker function followed by the wait-group decrement with no recover, which supports the earlier refutation. Because that premise comes from a dependency rather than the captured target, the account records the question as unresolved with the discriminator naming the wrapper body, rather than presenting the dependency reading as target evidence.\",\n \"answers\": {},\n \"knowledge\": [\n  {\n   \"entry_id\": \"599c7a85d804468187c5a652986302d1-knowledge-0\",\n   \"revision\": 1,\n   \"assertion\": \"A hard save